  <p>
    Honest Hank’s, a Tampa electrician referred by Rewired, went from about
    <strong>$25k to $80k average months</strong> in roughly eight months — 1 to 3 techs, third van
    wrapped — without a $10k day-one marketing package. They started on a roughly
    <strong>$500 Local Services Ads setup</strong> and only moved into more spend once that proved
    it could book work.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you’re an electrician being quoted a giant onboarding fee, this is the other model.
  </p>

  <h2>What does “stack small wins” mean for an electrician?</h2>
  <p>
    Lewis wasn’t starting from zero skill. He needed a company that looked and ran like one —
    consistent Google demand, brand that matched the work, crew capacity — without betting the shop
    on a fantasy retainer.
  </p>
  <p>
    John at Rewired referred him. That trust mattered: the relationship started with proof of how we
    grow companies in stages, not a slide deck. Full arc on the
    <a href="/case-studies/honest-hanks">Honest Hank’s case study</a>, including Lewis’s
    <a href="https://youtube.com/shorts/UtGKH_Aqv1U">video</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What came first (and what waited)?</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Google Business Profile + Local Services Ads + conversion basics.</strong> Tighten
      the profile, turn on LSA, fix high-impact presence. Those setups are usually a few hundred
      dollars and can add jobs fast — Hank’s ~$500 LSA setup is the published example.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Brand + website when lead flow justified it.</strong> A few thousand for looking like
      the company he was becoming — not an agency ransom note. See
      <a href="/services/branding">branding</a> and
      <a href="/services/websites">websites</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Vans, hiring, next channels.</strong> Van wrap, Facebook hiring tests, and the next
      lane only after the previous step was already making money.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <div class="receipt">
    <div class="receipt-label">THE SCOREBOARD</div>
    <p>
      ~$25k → $80k average monthly revenue. 1 → 3 techs. Third van wrapped and rolling. Built over
      about eight months — every step waiting on booked work.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why big setup fees burn electricians</h2>
  <p>
    A $10k onboarding package transfers all the risk to you before anyone has proven a thing. You’re
    funding someone else’s cash flow while your calendar is still empty. We wrote the pattern up in
    <a href="/blog/bad-vs-good-agency">bad vs good agencies</a>: cheap proof before expensive scale —
    LSA, profile, and site converting before Search spend ramps.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hank’s is that rule in one company. Search and bigger budgets arrived after the foundation
    booked jobs, not instead of it.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Every step waited on lead flow. Build something small → watch bookings rise → add the next piece.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>What electricians should copy from Hank’s</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Start with Keyword Planner truth and a correct GBP category (Motha’s lesson still applies).</li>
    <li>Buy LSA proof before a rebrand if cash is tight.</li>
    <li>Let brand and site follow revenue, not the other way around.</li>
    <li>Hire when trucks are the bottleneck — see the
      <a href="/blog/hiring-electricians-facebook-vs-indeed">Facebook vs Indeed hiring post</a>.</li>
    <li>
      Compare partners on published pricing —
      <a href="/get-started">ours is on get started</a> — and on sixty-day proof points, not
      guarantees.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Moments like van three aren’t a marketing slide. They’re the scoreboard for a process that never
    asked Lewis to fund a fantasy. If that sounds like the
    <a href="/for/electrician-marketing">electrician marketing</a> you want, start there.
  </p>
